  "_note": "FINE adv SWEEP -- characterises the penalty bands run 3 found. ANTENNA FITTED, -24 dBm.\n\nRun 3 measured adv {200,400,800}ms at 90.7% against {600,1200}ms at 68.1%, +22.6 points, p < 0.0001, with all 15 anchors at 20/20. But run 3 pinned broadcasts at 5, so adv and dwell were perfectly confounded (dwell = 5*adv): 'adv 600 is bad' and 'dwell 3000 is bad' fit that data equally well.\n\nIDENTIFIABILITY. broadcasts = dwell/adv, so only two of the three can be fixed -- there is no design that varies adv at both fixed dwell and fixed broadcasts. Fixing dwell instead would make broadcasts vary, and broadcast count is the largest known effect in the whole series (42.8% -> 97.2% in run 2), which would swamp everything. So broadcasts are pinned and TWO levels are run, 4 and 6:\n  - at a given adv, dwell differs between the arms (4*adv vs 6*adv)\n  - if the penalty tracks adv, it appears at the SAME adv in both arms\n  - if it tracks dwell, it appears at DIFFERENT adv in each arm\nThat is the separation run 3 could not make. Both levels sit below the ceiling at -24 dBm (run 2: bc4 = 83.3%, bc6 ~ 90%), so there is headroom in both directions.\n\nadv 200..1400ms in 100ms steps, 13 levels, spanning run 3's clean values (200/400/800) and both bad ones (600/1200). Resulting dwell is 800..8400ms; dwell is an established null across 1000..8000ms from runs 1 and 3, so it enters as a known-inert covariate.\n\nLeading hypothesis is BLE channel aliasing: advertising rotates across 37/38/39 while scanners dwell on one channel, and the spec's 0-10ms random per-event delay has little chance to average out over so few broadcasts. If true, the penalty should be periodic in adv rather than a single band -- which 100ms sampling can see and run 3's 5 coarse points could not.\n\n26 conditions x 6 reps = 156 cells + 12 anchors = 168 cells, 3360 keys. Anchors open and bisect every block (~7% of cells, matching the series convention) -- with a 22-point effect to confirm, they are the only thing separating a real band from an unusual hour. Condition order rotates by a stride of 7 per block so no condition holds a fixed position in the night.",
